Q1. Deadlock Debugging
Cause of Deadlock:
P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Both processes wait for each other, creating a circular wait and causing deadlock.
Optimization
Use a fixed order for resource allocation.
Example: Both processes should request R1 first and then R2.
Result:
Circular wait is eliminated.
Deadlock is avoided.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem
Cause:
Processes frequently move between Ready and Waiting due to repeated I/O operations.
The CPU stays idle while waiting for I/O completion.
Optimization:
Use multiprogramming to overlap CPU and I/O operations.
Apply efficient scheduling algorithms such as Round Robin or MLFQ.
Result:
Improved CPU utilization.
Reduced idle time.
Better throughput and response time.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging
Memory Usage:
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 25 × 1 MB = 25 MB
Total = 180 MB
Issue:
Too many threads increase stack memory usage and page faults, affecting performance.
Optimization:
Use a thread pool or reduce the number of threads.
Optimize memory allocation and reduce stack size.
Result:
Reduced page faults.
Improved memory utilization.
Faster execution.

Q4. Starvation Debugging
Cause:
P5 is placed in Q3 (lowest priority).
Higher-priority queues continue to receive CPU time, so P5 keeps waiting.
Optimization:
Apply Aging to gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
This ensures every process eventually gets CPU time.
Result:
Fair CPU scheduling.
Starvation is prevented.
Improved response for all processes.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Cause:
A very small time quantum (1 ms) causes frequent context switches.
Frequent I/O operations also increase switching overhead.
Optimization:
Increase the time quantum to about 5–10 ms.
Reduce unnecessary preemption and optimize I/O scheduling.
Result:
Lower context-switch overhead.
Better CPU efficiency.
Higher system throughput.
